1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,785
Part B starts with integration logic.

2
00:00:02,785 --> 00:00:03,914
The four steps:

3
00:00:03,914 --> 00:00:09,258
match a shared key, align the schemas, resolve conflicts, and re-audit.

4
00:00:09,258 --> 00:00:13,022
We split the file back into source A and source B.

5
00:00:13,022 --> 00:00:17,839
All of B's I Ds are found in A, and the I Ds are unique in each.

6
00:00:17,839 --> 00:00:25,968
Source A has the price column, source B has price U S D, so we rename it, and now the schemas are identical.

7
00:00:26,768 --> 00:00:27,493
Step three:

8
00:00:27,493 --> 00:00:31,449
merge on the key with an outer join and an indicator column.

9
00:00:31,449 --> 00:00:37,779
Forty seven thousand eight hundred ninety five rows are in A only, and one thousand are in both.

10
00:00:37,779 --> 00:00:46,878
Counting conflicts in the matched rows gives three hundred borough conflicts, three hundred room type conflicts, and zero price conflicts.

11
00:00:46,878 --> 00:00:51,032
They are case differences only, so we keep source A's spelling.

12
00:00:51,032 --> 00:00:56,768
The result is forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five rows with zero duplicates.

13
00:00:57,568 --> 00:00:59,753
Duplicates come in three kinds.

14
00:00:59,753 --> 00:01:03,277
Exact duplicates, where every column is identical:

15
00:01:03,277 --> 00:01:04,616
seven hundred rows.

16
00:01:04,616 --> 00:01:07,646
Same key duplicates, where the I D repeats:

17
00:01:07,646 --> 00:01:13,425
one thousand rows, the seven hundred copies plus the three hundred lowercase rows.

18
00:01:13,425 --> 00:01:14,835
And near-duplicates:

19
00:01:14,835 --> 00:01:22,305
lowercase and trim the name, collapse extra spaces, and look for the same host with the same cleaned name.

20
00:01:22,305 --> 00:01:28,648
That gives four hundred eighty one candidates, which should be reviewed, not auto-deleted.

21
00:01:29,448 --> 00:01:33,034
Rows and columns need two different one-line fixes.

22
00:01:33,034 --> 00:01:36,339
For rows, drop duplicates keeps the first copy:

23
00:01:36,339 --> 00:01:43,793
rows go from forty nine thousand eight hundred ninety five to forty nine thousand one hundred ninety five.

24
00:01:43,793 --> 00:01:50,051
For columns, transpose, find duplicates, and keep the non-duplicate columns with a tilde:

25
00:01:50,051 --> 00:01:53,918
seventeen columns become sixteen, dropping price U S D.

26
00:01:53,918 --> 00:02:00,528
Three hundred repeated I Ds remain, because the lowercase copies are not yet exact duplicates.

27
00:02:01,328 --> 00:02:05,200
Standardise categories across the whole column at once.

28
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:13,506
Strip whitespace and apply title case to the borough, capitalise the room type, and collapse extra spaces in the name.

29
00:02:13,506 --> 00:02:19,560
Distinct borough labels drop from ten to five, and room type labels from six to three.

30
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:23,432
For messy codes, use a mapping dictionary with replace.

31
00:02:24,232 --> 00:02:28,108
Standardising created three hundred new exact duplicates.

32
00:02:28,108 --> 00:02:36,064
Brooklyn in lowercase is not equal to Brooklyn in title case, and that hid three hundred copies from drop duplicates.

33
00:02:36,064 --> 00:02:40,348
After title case they became exact duplicates, so we drop them:

34
00:02:40,348 --> 00:02:48,984
forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five true unique listings, identical to the correctly integrated table from part six.

35
00:02:48,984 --> 00:02:53,608
Order matters, so the audit must run again after each cleaning step.

36
00:02:54,408 --> 00:02:58,327
Now one reusable rule table to re-run on every load.

37
00:02:58,327 --> 00:03:04,659
Range rules like price at least zero and availability from zero to three sixty five.

38
00:03:04,659 --> 00:03:07,598
Type rules like price being an integer.

39
00:03:07,598 --> 00:03:10,538
Uniqueness rules like I D being unique.

40
00:03:10,538 --> 00:03:14,683
And set rules like room type being in the allowed list.

41
00:03:14,683 --> 00:03:17,472
All twelve rules pass after cleaning.

42
00:03:18,272 --> 00:03:20,320
Standardisation for scale.

43
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:21,029
Step one:

44
00:03:21,029 --> 00:03:24,652
measure skew for every numeric column at once.

45
00:03:24,652 --> 00:03:29,063
Price has a skew of nineteen, minimum nights twenty two.

46
00:03:29,063 --> 00:03:37,333
You can log all skewed columns in one line using log one p, adding a suffix, and joining the result back.

47
00:03:37,333 --> 00:03:41,744
After the log, price skew drops to zero point five five.

48
00:03:42,544 --> 00:03:46,563
Here is log one p taming the long right tail of price.

49
00:03:46,563 --> 00:03:52,592
The raw price histogram is crushed against zero with skew nineteen point one two.

50
00:03:52,592 --> 00:03:58,694
After log one p, the shape is much more symmetric, with skew zero point five five.

51
00:03:58,694 --> 00:04:03,160
Log of one plus x is safe for the eleven zero-dollar prices.

52
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:04,614
Step two:

53
00:04:04,614 --> 00:04:12,613
Standard Scaler puts every feature on one scale, with z equal to x minus the mean over the standard deviation.

54
00:04:12,613 --> 00:04:23,519
Price had a mean of one fifty two and a standard deviation of two forty before; after scaling every z column has mean zero and standard deviation one.

55
00:04:23,519 --> 00:04:29,627
Do not scale identifiers and coordinates like I D, host I D, latitude and longitude.

56
00:04:29,627 --> 00:04:35,880
And log first, then scale, so outliers do not set the new mean and standard deviation.

57
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:39,389
Here is the whole lab in one table.

58
00:04:39,389 --> 00:04:39,776
Rows:

59
00:04:39,776 --> 00:04:47,982
forty nine thousand eight hundred ninety five raw, forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five cleaned.

60
00:04:47,982 --> 00:04:50,846
Duplicate rows seven hundred to zero.

61
00:04:50,846 --> 00:04:53,168
Duplicate columns one to zero.

62
00:04:53,168 --> 00:04:55,877
Repeated I Ds one thousand to zero.

63
00:04:55,877 --> 00:04:57,967
Borough labels ten to five.

64
00:04:57,967 --> 00:05:00,290
Room type labels six to three.

65
00:05:00,290 --> 00:05:04,779
Price skew nineteen to zero point five five after the log.

66
00:05:04,779 --> 00:05:08,108
And twelve of twelve validation rules pass.

67
00:05:08,108 --> 00:05:08,960
Still open:

68
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:17,552
missing values in the next session, and the flagged zero and ten thousand dollar prices in the outlier session.

69
00:05:18,352 --> 00:05:24,026
The same code works on a second dataset, Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention:

70
00:05:24,026 --> 00:05:31,737
twelve thousand three hundred thirty rows, zero missing cells, and one hundred twenty five duplicate rows.

71
00:05:31,737 --> 00:05:32,683
Two findings.

72
00:05:32,683 --> 00:05:33,556
Consistency:

73
00:05:33,556 --> 00:05:39,885
nine months use three-letter codes, but June is spelled out, so we replace it with Jun.

74
00:05:39,885 --> 00:05:40,831
Completeness:

75
00:05:40,831 --> 00:05:44,686
zero empty cells, yet January and April never appear.

76
00:05:44,686 --> 00:05:46,432
Coverage gaps count too.

77
00:05:47,232 --> 00:05:51,243
On the shoppers data, validity rules show zero failures.

78
00:05:51,243 --> 00:06:01,342
For accuracy, twenty two sessions spent more than four hours on product pages, with a maximum of seventeen point eight hours, worth flagging.

79
00:06:01,342 --> 00:06:06,498
After dropping duplicates we have twelve thousand two hundred five rows.

80
00:06:06,498 --> 00:06:07,788
And watch the log:

81
00:06:07,788 --> 00:06:16,526
product related duration skew goes from seven point two five to minus one point four three, so log one p can over-correct.

82
00:06:16,526 --> 00:06:19,104
Re-check skew after every transform.

83
00:06:19,904 --> 00:06:20,982
Key takeaways.

84
00:06:20,982 --> 00:06:27,680
Audit first, with shape, isnull sum, duplicated sum and the transposed duplicate check.

85
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:35,996
Validity is checked against rules, accuracy against reality; the ten thousand dollar room passes every rule.

86
00:06:35,996 --> 00:06:41,847
Completeness includes why data is missing and which categories never appear.

87
00:06:41,847 --> 00:06:47,083
Integrate on a key, align schemas, resolve conflicts, then re-audit.

88
00:06:47,083 --> 00:06:53,550
Standardising labels can create new duplicates, so re-run the audit after every fix.

89
00:06:53,550 --> 00:06:56,476
And log first, standard scaler second.

90
00:06:56,476 --> 00:06:58,016
Thanks for watching.

